  {
    "episode_id": 70922,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/0/70922.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 26,
    "turn_text": "I think it's a combination of people who believe the echo chamber and that the Trump inspired love of Putin and respect for Putin. It's anti-establishment. No, we've all been taught to hate Russia, but Russia's good and Putin's leading in a real way. Part of it is that. Part of it is, you know, that people are the economic argument. They don't want to be spending money. They are isolationists, right? They don't want a strong American role in the world. And by the way, I feel that from the left and the right, right? I get heckled now in parades in Michigan from both the left and the right to get out of Ukraine, no more money to Ukraine. So there's a strong isolationist feeling in many people in the country. And then I think, you know, then I can't explain it. I honestly don't understand how anyone who was raised in, again, post-World War II America, post-Cold War America, can sit there and, like, wax poetically about Vladimir Putin. Like, that guy is a dangerous dude. and not good for American interests. So it is confusing for me as a national security person by training to hear my peers, almost glorifying Putin in the House of Representatives.",
