    "turn_text": "a bent rim. So I went to the mechanic and I'm waiting. And of course I'm making calls and I'm doing work while I'm waiting to get my rim fixed. And a guy came up to me and it is, I could have predicted what he was going to engage me on. He worked at the, you know, bell tire. And he's like, I just got to tell you, like, I just, it is so hard to afford my medication. I don't know. Is there anything you can do? I don't know what to tell my wife. I mean, I can't tell you the number of times people come up to me, clutch my arm and say, I can't afford my insulin or I can't afford my whatever that is. I would say our healthcare system, including the cost of prescription drugs, is broken. It is broken. And you can argue as we should about what a better system looks like, but we can't shy away from the fact that people are paying way too much of their annual income to health care and prescription drugs. We've tried to do something about that. the Democrats in the House and the Senate have tried, we lowered the cross of insulin. We're doing as much as we can, but the system is creating perverse incentives to charge people desperate amounts of money for life-saving drugs, and it's just, it is untenable for most of Americans.",
